import{_ as i,P as a,d as n,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.24 Population, Generations, the Life Course, and Living Across Places","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.24 Population, Generations, the Life Course, and Living Across Places","xkChars":0,"xkWords":556,"xkLessons":{"D.24":{"title":"D.24 Population, Generations, the Life Course, and Life Across Places","lead":"Separate population totals, composition, life courses, and personal choices. Even a shared version label within a data family does not guarantee synchronized updates across tables.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5376–5391, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-24#section-d-24. See the theory-v3.0 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘An identity is not a policy goal.’ Population stocks change through births, deaths, immigration, and emigration. The accounting relationship only shows how quantities connect; it does not decide what population size should be sought, much less authorize assigning reproductive duties over individual bodies. The conditions for having children, not having children, and raising children safely each pose normative questions. [T-main]","Crude rates are affected by population structure. Age-specific rates, period total measures, and actual cohort outcomes are distinct objects. Zero net migration can conceal large inflows and outflows. Nor can an age structure be converted directly into each household's care hours or an institution's fiscal burden; actual support networks and institutional conditions must be added. [T-main]","Age, period, and cohort satisfy age = period − birth cohort. The resulting linear identification problem does not disappear merely by adding observations. Specific stage-related shocks, curvature, or comparisons under additional assumptions can still be studied; an identification limitation must not be rewritten as the impossibility of all life-course research. [T-main]","Early experiences may affect later outcomes through present skills and resources. Controlling for these mediators changes the question being estimated. An insignificant or zero historical conditional increment does not prove history never mattered. Conversely, the existence of historical effects does not mean the future cannot change. [T-main]","Migration analysis separates legal status, aspirations, practical feasibility, and compulsion; conditions of choice can change during the process. Someone forced to leave may still have choices at the destination or in particular matters, while lacking alternatives at specific stages. One free/forced label cannot fill in every stage. [T-main]","The ILO's explanation of voluntariness requires both free and informed acceptance of work and freedom to leave. A past signature alone therefore cannot prove continuing voluntariness: this is a reasonable limiting inference from the definition. It does not mean that lack of a signed contract or difficult circumstances alone establish forced labor; other elements and conditions of applicability still require checking. [P-ILO]","Members of the same family may separately bear remittance duties, care work, status constraints, and distance constraints. Succession likewise involves distinct transfers of knowledge, office, resources, decision-making powers, and responsibility. Age does not naturally confer authority. A new generation can revise old arrangements; preserving memory does not mean repeating an old way of life. [T-main]","In the source's organizational example, total membership remains unchanged while experienced people leave, newcomers join, and care needs grow. A constant headcount is only one quantitative outcome. It cannot establish complete continuity of knowledge or unchanged burdens; composition, skills, handovers, and support conditions must be recorded. [T-main]","Real versioning case: the UN Population Division's January 19, 2026 interim update to WPP2024 explains that Togo's published census figures had already been adjusted for net undercount, and the Division had applied another adjustment. The correction covers Togo's 1950–2023 estimates and medium-variant population and vital-event counts for 2024–2100. Fertility and mortality rates, other scenarios, other locations, and global and regional aggregates were not changed in tandem. [P-UN]","Recording only WPP2024 therefore does not fix the data's state. Record the specific file, revision identity, download date, country, and scenario. This is a research cross-checking recommendation, not a claim that the UN mandates a universal operating standard. Without recalculating tables cell by cell, do not add unverified global discrepancies. [P-UN]","A population turning point requires distinguishing the first decline, the beginning of a sustained decline, and the year when the sustained-decline criterion is confirmed; natural decrease is separate again. Falling public expenditure does not automatically mean falling need: burdens may shift to households or nonresident supporters. Connect to §§5.5.1 and 5.5.4 for aggregation and identification, §6.12 and §9.2 for history and current conditions, and §10.7 and §15.2.5 for transmission and updating across positions. [T-main]","The eight research entry points are: 24.01, fertility, mortality, and demographic transition; 24.02, age structure and care mechanisms; 24.03, age–period–cohort identification; 24.04, critical periods and cumulative paths; 24.05, migration status, aspirations, and feasibility; 24.06, distinct handovers in inheritance and organizational succession; 24.07, normative boundaries of population policy and reproductive justice; and 24.08, experiences of leaving home, separation, belonging, and life across places. Navigation cannot replace original evidence in each field. [T-main]","Conditions of choice in migration, labor, and life across places can change through the process. A previous contract is evidence about that time. If an arrangement later creates debt, ties housing to it, or establishes dependence on care, remaining in place becomes weaker evidence of continuing voluntariness. For the matter currently at issue, check refusal, negotiation, exit, and limited responsibility separately. Do not equate local agency with complete consent or remove those unable to exit from the factual record."],"argumentSteps":["First identify quantities, composition, time windows, and statistical objects.","Separate flow identities from normative policy aims.","Check identification assumptions for period, cohort, and age, and the target of controlling for mediators.","Check migration intentions, feasibility, coercion, and powers at each stage.","Record the scope of revisions across tables and trace actual handovers of burdens and knowledge."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Net migration","leftText":"In-migration minus out-migration.","rightTitle":"Gross movement","rightText":"In-migration and out-migration can both be large."},{"leftTitle":"Period measure","leftText":"An aggregation of rates for a specified period.","rightTitle":"Cohort outcome","rightText":"A group's actual life course; not automatically interchangeable with a period measure."},{"leftTitle":"Age relationship","leftText":"Age = period − cohort.","rightTitle":"Independent identification","rightText":"Requires additional structure or assumptions, not simply more observations."},{"leftTitle":"Version family","leftText":"For example, WPP2024.","rightTitle":"Actual revision scope","rightText":"Check files, locations, years, and scenarios individually."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: headcount is unchanged while experience leaves and care needs increase.","Source boundary: a historical residual absorbed by current mediators does not prove that earlier effects never occurred.","Teaching counterexample: 100 move in and 100 move out; the net is zero, but gross directional flows total 200."],"questions":["Does zero net migration mean nobody moved?","Does a larger sample eliminate the linear age–period–cohort relationship?","Does the same version number guarantee synchronized tables?","Can fertility policy goals directly determine individual obligations?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/figures/d24-flow.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The UN's 2026 interim correction to WPP2024 for Togo","href":"https://population.un.org/wpp/assets/Files/WPP2024_Release-Note-rev1.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The UN's 2026 interim correction to WPP2024 for Togo","text":"The 2026-01-19 note explains the duplicate net-undercount adjustment and limited revision scope. Only specified estimates and medium-variant counts for Togo were corrected; other scenarios, locations, and global and regional aggregates were not updated in tandem.","sourceLabel":"UN Population Division: World Population Prospects 2024 Interim Update","sourceUrl":"https://population.un.org/wpp/assets/Files/WPP2024_Release-Note-rev1.pdf","sourceLocator":"2026-01-19; page 1, the four explanations in §2 and file list in §3; page 2, rev.1 marking","boundary":"Only the two-page release note was checked; the data were not recalculated cell by cell. A shared version-family label cannot establish that every table has the same state.","href":"https://population.un.org/wpp/assets/Files/WPP2024_Release-Note-rev1.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.24","start_line":5432,"end_line":5447,"startLine":5432,"endLine":5447}],"outputId":"section-d-24","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: net zero does not mean no migration","text":"An artificial population starts at 1000, with 20 births, 20 deaths, 100 arrivals, and 100 departures, and ends at 1000. Net migration is 0, while total inflow plus outflow is 200. An unchanged stock does not imply unchanged membership or knowledge.","assumptions":"One time window and complete population accounting; a gross flow of 200 does not guarantee 200 distinct individuals.","boundary":"The numbers do not establish specific migration motives, coercion, or policy aims."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the start of a turn versus its confirmation","text":"Artificial year-end populations are 100, 99, 98, and 97. A decline is first observed in year two. If confirmation requires three consecutive declines, as specified in advance, the continuous process is confirmed only in year four, while its beginning remains year two.","assumptions":"Four purely artificial years with a confirmation rule specified beforehand.","boundary":"The confirmation year is not treated as the year of natural decrease; no decomposition into births and deaths is provided."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: headcount is unchanged while experience leaves and care needs increase.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: a historical residual absorbed by current mediators does not prove that earlier effects never occurred.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: 100 move in and 100 move out; the net is zero, but gross directional flows total 200.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does zero net migration mean nobody moved?","answer":"No. Inflows and outflows can offset one another.","basis":"Stocks and flows."},{"question":"Does a larger sample eliminate the linear age–period–cohort relationship?","answer":"No. Identification assumptions remain necessary, while other questions can still be studied.","basis":"Identification."},{"question":"Does the same version number guarantee synchronized tables?","answer":"No. Check revision scope file by file.","basis":"The real-world case."},{"question":"Can fertility policy goals directly determine individual obligations?","answer":"Statistical goals do not own individual bodies; normative reasons require a separate argument.","basis":"Boundaries of authority."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/figures/d24-flow.png","alt":"Births, deaths, arrivals, and departures can offset: the total stays fixed while movement and composition change.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/figures/d24-version.png","alt":"The UN's 2026 interim update covers specified parts of Togo's data; other scenarios and aggregates were not synchronized.","caption":"Based on the release note."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/figures/d24-time.png","alt":"The linear age–period–cohort relationship; confirmation time differs from the beginning of the process.","caption":"A teaching relationship and construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures explain stocks and flows, the UN's partial update, and age–period–cohort relations.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["WPP data were not compared table by table, and no global discrepancy was estimated.","No assessment was made of a particular migrant's legal status or conditions of choice."],"taskId":"S-D-24","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.24, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4901–4916","claim_support":["Population-flow identification, migration choices, succession, normative boundaries, turning points, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-UN","title":"UN Population Division: World Population Prospects 2024 Interim Update","url":"https://population.un.org/wpp/assets/Files/WPP2024_Release-Note-rev1.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2026-01-19，WPP2024 rev.1","locator":"2026-01-19; page 1, the four explanations in §2 and file list in §3; page 2, rev.1 marking","claim_support":["A duplicate net-undercount adjustment to Togo's census figures prompted the correction. Only the specified country-years and medium-variant counts were updated; other scenarios, locations, and global and regional aggregates were not."],"limits":["The lesson author read the two-page release note in full but did not compare every item in the data ZIP. The note is not treated as evidence that all files or scenarios were revised in tandem."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-ILO","title":"ILO: What is forced labour?","url":"https://www.ilo.org/topics-and-sectors/forced-labour-modern-slavery-and-trafficking-persons/what-forced-labour","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"The international legal definition of forced labour: definition and the third of its three elements (web-page lines 54–63); qualifications on exceptions, lines 74–80","claim_support":["Voluntariness includes free and informed acceptance of work and freedom to leave; a single signature cannot replace checking conditions throughout the process."],"limits":["The lesson author read the definition and its context. ‘A previous signature is insufficient’ is a limiting inference from the definition, not a sentence quoted from the page. No particular contract or case is judged; forced labor also requires other elements and applicable rules."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"D.24　人口、代际、生命历程与跨地生活; Chinese source lines 5376–5391; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/source/S-D-24.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-24/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e78258f0734c7acf51e335d41bcc40b6447bb2670191793bd96ee8e6981feb07","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-24.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-24.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-24.md"};function l(u,e,h,p,m,g){const t=a("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-24" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.24</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Population, Generations, the Life Course, and Living Across Places <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-24" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.24 Population, Generations, the Life Course, and Living Across Places {#section-d-24}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Separate population totals, composition, life courses, and personal choices. Even a shared version label within a data family does not guarantee synchronized updates across tables.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §5.5.1 and 5.5.4, §6.12, §9.2, §10.7, §14.5, and §15.2.5. Research entries cover 24.01–24.08.</p><p>Population stocks change through flows such as births, deaths, in-migration, and out-migration. The identity does not supply a policy objective. Crude rates, age-specific rates, aggregate period measures, and actual cohort outcomes are distinct. Net migration can be zero even when many people move in and many move out. Age structure cannot be translated directly into actual care and fiscal burdens for each family or institution. Comparisons across files also require checking what was actually revised. Under the same version number, country tables, aggregates, and scenarios may not all have been updated together (United Nations Population Division, 2026).</p><p>Age, period, and cohort satisfy a specific linear relation. More observations cannot by themselves eliminate the resulting linear identification problem. Shocks at particular stages, curvature, and comparisons that make additional assumptions can still be studied. Early experiences may affect later outcomes through current resources and skills. If current conditions fully absorb the historical residual, that does not mean earlier effects never occurred or that change is impossible in the future.</p><p>Analysis of migration must distinguish legal status, wishes, actual feasibility, and coercion. Conditions for choice may change over the course of the process. Having signed a contract does not establish continued voluntariness (ILO, see the definition of “voluntariness”). People forced to leave may still have choices in some respects and no feasible alternatives in others. When explaining recruitment, labor, settlement, or return, check the relevant conditions separately for the current question. Members of the same family may face different constraints involving remittances, care, status, and geography. Succession involves knowledge, positions, resources, decision-making authority, and responsibility. Age does not confer authority by itself. A new generation can revise old arrangements. Preserving memory does not require repeating the old way of life.</p><p>In a teaching example, membership in an organization remains the same in number, but experienced people leave, new people enter, and care needs grow. Treating the headcount as evidence of “complete continuity” would miss changes in knowledge and burden. The shared goals of population policy do not give it ownership of individual bodies. Having children, not having children, and having conditions for raising children safely should be stated as separate normative questions. Statistical needs do not justify assigning duties to individuals.</p><p>The year of a population turning point depends on the question. The first decline, the first year of a continuous decline, the year a sustained decline is confirmed, and natural decrease do not designate the same point in time. When early experience affects later outcomes through current resources, controlling for those mediators changes the estimand. The disappearance of a historical conditional increment does not establish that the earlier experience never mattered. Lower public expenditure does not establish lower needs. Check whether burdens shifted to families and people who provide support from outside the household.</p><p>Research topics. 24.01 Demographic transition, fertility, and mortality. 24.02 Age structure, aging, and care. 24.03 Cohort, period, and age effects. 24.04 The life course, critical periods, and cumulative advantage. 24.05 Migrants, refugees, diasporas, and boundaries. 24.06 Generational succession, inheritance, and transmission within organizations. 24.07 Population policy and reproductive justice. 24.08 Dispersal, leaving home, belonging, and living across places.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.24"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=i(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
